(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-longtermcare"]={"plain":{"bills":{"longtermcare":{"slug":"longtermcare","title":"The Long-Term Care Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as The Long-Term Care Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":null,"body":"The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** Medicaid is the largest payer for long-term services and supports in the United States.\n\n**(2)** Coverage of nursing facility services is required of every State plan. Coverage of services furnished in an individual's own home is, with narrow exceptions, optional. **The preference for institutional care is not a budgetary accident; it is written into the statute.**\n\n**(3)** Because home and community-based services are optional, they are furnished under waiver authorities that permit a State to cap the number of people served, to serve some parts of a State and not others, and to serve some groups and not others. Waiting periods for services are the predictable result of authorities designed to permit them.\n\n**(4)** An individual who qualifies for a nursing facility is entitled to that care. The same individual, seeking the same level of assistance at home, may be placed on a list. **The entitlement follows the setting rather than the person.**\n\n**(5)** Unpaid family caregiving is a substantial and largely unmeasured subsidy to the long-term care system. Where formal services are unavailable, the cost is borne by family members who reduce their hours of paid work, leave employment, or provide care beyond their capacity.\n\n**(6)** Direct care work is among the lowest paid occupations in the United States, and turnover in that workforce is a principal cause of interrupted care.\n\n**(7)** Federal law has for more than two decades required that services be furnished in the most integrated setting appropriate to the needs of the individual. **That requirement has been enforced case by case against a financing structure that points the other way.**\n\n**(8)** **Where care at home meets an individual's needs as safely as care in a facility and costs no more, there is no reason of policy, and none of cost, to pay for the facility instead.** That proposition does not depend on which program is paying, and this Act therefore applies it to Medicare, to Medicaid, and to private coverage alike.\n\n**(9)** **The apparent cheapness of care at home is frequently an accounting artifact.** Where a family member leaves paid employment to furnish care without payment, the cost has not been reduced; it has been moved onto that person and out of the measured system.\n\n**(10)** **This Act widens a choice and narrows none.** Care in a nursing facility remains covered for every individual who qualifies for it and wants it. **Nothing in this Act permits a payer to answer a request for institutional care by pointing to a cheaper plan of care at home.**\n\n**(11)** **Where an individual who would prefer to be at home is instead in a facility, two people are worse off: that individual, and the person who needs the bed.** Facility capacity is scarce, unevenly distributed, and in much of the country contracting. **A rule that lets people who prefer home care receive it therefore does two things at once \u2014 it widens the choice available to the individual, and it frees capacity for those who need or prefer a facility.**\n\n**(12)** **This Act is not a program of deinstitutionalization.** It closes no facility, reduces no facility's payment, and moves no one. **It changes only what a payer must cover when a person who qualifies for a facility would rather be at home.**"},{"anchor":"sec-19-1","heading":"\u00a7 19-1. Definitions","part":null,"body":"**(a) HOME AND COMMUNITY-BASED SERVICES.** \u2014 Personal care and attendant services, homemaker services, home health aide services, habilitation, respite, day services, supported employment, case management, and assistive technology, furnished to an individual in the individual's own home or in a community setting that is not an institution.\n\n**(a-1) RESIDENTIAL SETTINGS ARE WITHIN THE DEFINITION.** \u2014 A residential setting that is not an institution \u2014 including assisted living, a group home, and adult foster care \u2014 is a community setting for purposes of subsection (a). **The requirements applicable to such a setting are those already applied under the federal home and community-based settings regulation, and this component imposes no others.**\n\n**(b) INSTITUTION.** \u2014 A nursing facility, an intermediate care facility for individuals with intellectual disabilities, an institution for mental diseases, or a hospital.\n\n**(c) DIRECT CARE WORKER.** \u2014 An individual furnishing personal care, attendant, homemaker, home health aide, or habilitation services directly to an individual, whether employed by a provider, by a fiscal intermediary, or by the individual receiving services.\n\n**(d) FAMILY CAREGIVER.** \u2014 A relative of the individual by blood, marriage, or adoption, or a person standing in a comparable relationship, who furnishes care to the individual. **A spouse and a legally responsible relative are within this definition.**\n\n**(e) REQUEST.** \u2014 A communication by or on behalf of an individual, in any form, indicating that the individual seeks home and community-based services. **No particular form, document, or channel may be required for a communication to be a request, and the date of the request is the date the State first received it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-19-1a","heading":"\u00a7 19-1A. The rule \u2014 care at home that is as safe and costs no more is covered","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE RULE.** \u2014 Where a payer covers services furnished to an individual in an institution, the payer shall cover services furnished in the individual's home or in a community setting if \u2014\n\n**(1)** the services meet the needs established by the individual's assessment **as safely** as the institutional services would; and\n\n**(2)** the cost of those services **does not exceed** the cost of the institutional services.\n\n**(a-1) ELIGIBILITY IS THE SAME ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 An individual eligible for the institutional services is eligible for services under this section. **A payer may not apply to services furnished at home any eligibility criterion, threshold, assessment instrument, scoring method, diagnosis requirement, or age requirement that it does not apply to the institutional services.**\n\n**(b) PAYER.** \u2014 For purposes of this section, a payer is the Medicare program, a State plan under title XIX, a qualified health plan offered through an Exchange, a group health plan, and a health insurance issuer offering group or individual coverage.\n\n**(c) THE COMPARISON IS BETWEEN PLANS OF CARE, OVER THE SAME PERIOD.** \u2014 The cost compared under subsection (a)(2) is the total the payer would pay for the plan of care at home over a period, against the total the payer would pay for the institutional services over that same period. **An individual service may not be compared against an institutional day, and no comparison may be made against a setting for which the individual does not qualify.**\n\n**(c-1) WHAT THE COST OF CARE AT HOME COMPRISES \u2014 AND WHAT IS THEREFORE COVERED.** \u2014 The cost compared under subsection (c) comprises, and the services covered under this section include \u2014\n\n**(1)** labor, including compensation of direct care workers, skilled nursing, therapy, and care coordination;\n\n**(2)** medical and personal care supplies and consumables;\n\n**(3)** durable medical equipment and assistive technology;\n\n**(4)** remote monitoring and telehealth, **including the devices, connectivity, and technical support required to use them**;\n\n**(5)** modification of the dwelling to the extent necessary to furnish the services safely;\n\n**(6)** transportation necessary to obtain a covered service; and\n\n**(7)** training of the individual and of any person furnishing the individual's care.\n\n**(c-2) AN ITEM COUNTED IN THE COMPARISON IS A COVERED SERVICE.** \u2014 **A payer may not count an item under subsection (c-1) as a cost of care at home and then decline to pay for it.** Where a payer includes an item in the comparison, that item is covered; where a payer declines to cover an item, it may not be counted against the individual in the comparison.\n\n**(c-3) NO OMISSION EITHER.** \u2014 The comparison shall include every item under subsection (c-1) that the assessment establishes is needed. **A comparison computed from labor alone does not satisfy this section.**\n\n**(c-4) THE INSTITUTIONAL SIDE OF THE COMPARISON IS A PUBLISHED RATE.** \u2014 The cost of the institutional services is the payer's applicable published rate for the setting for which the individual qualifies, for the period compared. **It is not computed case by case, and a payer may not substitute an estimate, an average of lower-cost settings, or a rate it does not in fact pay.**\n\n**(d) UNPAID LABOR IS NOT A SAVING.** \u2014 The cost of care at home shall be computed from the services the assessment establishes are needed, **without regard to care a family member or other person may be willing to furnish without payment.** A plan of care is not less costly by reason of an expectation that a person will work without being paid.\n\n**(e) WHO DETERMINES SAFETY.** \u2014 Whether services meet the individual's needs as safely is determined by the assessment under section 19-4 and by the clinician responsible for the individual's care. **A payer may not substitute its own judgment for that determination.**\n\n**(f) A DENIAL IS CONDITIONED, AND THE CONDITION IS A WRITING.** \u2014 A payer may determine that subsection (a)(1) is not met only by a writing that identifies the specific need that cannot be met as safely at home, states the facts on which that rests, and is furnished to the individual and to the clinician. **Where no such writing has been made, the condition in subsection (a)(1) is met**, and the payer has no authority to deny on that ground.\n\n**(f-1) AND IT IS REVIEWABLE.** \u2014 A determination under subsection (f), and any determination reducing or terminating services under this component, is subject to the payer's existing process for reviewing an adverse benefit determination. **Where the determination reduces or terminates services the individual is receiving, those services continue unchanged until the review is decided.** **No new tribunal is created; the machinery each payer already operates is used.**\n\n**(g) WHERE THE COST COMPARISON IS CLOSE OR UNCERTAIN, IT IS MET.** \u2014 The condition in subsection (a)(2) is satisfied where the cost of care at home does not exceed the cost of the institutional services by more than the band stated in the schedule, or where the cost of the institutional services cannot be determined.\n\n**(h) THE ROUTES AROUND THIS, CLOSED.** \u2014 A payer may not defeat this section by any other means, including a prior authorization requirement, a network or provider limitation, a limit expressed in visits, hours, or episodes, a benefit category or setting-of-care definition, or a characterization of the services as custodial, maintenance, long-term, or non-skilled \u2014 **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the measure.** **The enumeration in this subsection is illustrative and not limiting.**\n\n**(h-1) THE BARRIERS THAT DO NOT APPLY TO SERVICES COVERED UNDER THIS SECTION.** \u2014 Whatever the payer, none of the following applies to services covered under this section \u2014\n\n**(1)** an exclusion or limitation of care characterized as custodial, maintenance, long-term, personal, or non-skilled;\n\n**(2)** a requirement that the individual be confined to the home, be unable to leave it without assistance, or be homebound by any measure;\n\n**(3)** a limit expressed in visits, hours, days, or episodes, **except to the extent the individual's assessment establishes the services needed**;\n\n**(4)** a requirement of a prior inpatient, institutional, or hospital stay of any duration; and\n\n**(5)** a requirement that services be intermittent, part-time, short-term, or restorative, or that the individual be expected to improve.\n\n**Paragraph (5) applies notwithstanding any contrary rule, coverage policy, or standard of any payer. The omission of a barrier from this subsection is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(i) THIS SECTION OPERATES IN ONE DIRECTION ONLY.** \u2014 This section **adds** coverage and may never subtract it.\n\n**(1)** Nothing in this section authorizes a payer to deny, reduce, delay, or condition coverage of services furnished in an institution.\n\n**(2)** **The comparison required by subsections (c) through (c-4) may not be used, in any form, as a ground for denying institutional services.** That a plan of care at home would meet the individual's needs as safely, or would cost less, is **not** a permissible basis for denying, reducing, or terminating coverage of care in an institution.\n\n**(3)** A payer may not require an individual to attempt, trial, or first receive care at home as a condition of coverage of institutional services.\n\n**(4)** **Where an individual qualifies for institutional services, the individual's choice of setting governs**, and a payer may not disadvantage the individual for choosing either setting.\n\n**(5)** No individual may be required to receive care at home, to accept a particular caregiver, or to accept monitoring or a device in the individual's dwelling.\n\n**(j) WHAT THE INDIVIDUAL PAYS MAY NOT EXCEED WHAT THE INSTITUTION WOULD HAVE COST THEM.**\n\n**(1) AGGREGATE, NOT SERVICE BY SERVICE.** \u2014 The total of all amounts payable by the individual for the plan of care at home over a period \u2014 deductibles, coinsurance, copayments, and any amount payable for an item described in subsection (c-1) \u2014 **may not exceed the total the individual would have paid for the institutional services over that same period.**\n\n**(2) THE PROGRAM'S SAVING MAY NOT BE FINANCED BY THE INDIVIDUAL.** \u2014 Where the plan of care at home costs the payer less than the institutional services, **the individual's out-of-pocket cost may not exceed what it would have been in the institution.** A reduction in what the payer spends may not appear as an increase in what the individual spends.\n\n**(3) THE EXCESS IS NOT COLLECTIBLE.** \u2014 An amount above the limit in paragraph (1) is not payable by the individual, and may not be billed to the individual, collected, referred for collection, or reported to a consumer reporting agency.\n\n**(4) MEDICAID \u2014 THE PERSON AT HOME PAYS RENT.** \u2014 In applying the post-eligibility treatment of income under title XIX to an individual receiving services at home, the amount the individual retains shall be not less than the amount necessary to maintain the individual's housing, utilities, and food.\n\n**(5) NO OTHER CHARGE.** \u2014 No provider may impose any charge for a covered service beyond the amount permitted by this subsection, and a term of any agreement purporting to permit otherwise is void."},{"anchor":"sec-19-1b","heading":"\u00a7 19-1B. Application to Medicare","part":null,"body":"**(a) EXTENDED CARE.** \u2014 Where an individual is entitled to post-hospital extended care services, the Secretary shall cover services furnished in the individual's home meeting the conditions of section 19-1A.\n\n**(b) THE PROVISIONS THAT DO NOT APPLY.** \u2014 Section 19-1A(h-1) applies to the Medicare program, and in particular the exclusion of custodial care under section 1862(a)(9) of the Social Security Act, the requirement that an individual be confined to the home under sections 1814(a)(2)(C) and 1835(a)(2)(A) of that Act, and any limitation expressed as intermittent or part-time, do not apply to services covered under this section.\n\n**(c) QUALIFYING STAY.** \u2014 Coverage under this section does not depend on a prior inpatient stay of any duration."},{"anchor":"sec-19-1c","heading":"\u00a7 19-1C. Application to Exchange and group coverage","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE REQUIREMENT.** \u2014 A qualified health plan offered through an Exchange, a group health plan, and a health insurance issuer offering group or individual health insurance coverage that covers services furnished in a skilled nursing facility, a rehabilitation facility, or any other institutional setting shall cover services furnished in the individual's home meeting the conditions of section 19-1A.\n\n**(b) NOT A STATE-MANDATED BENEFIT.** \u2014 The requirement of this section is a Federal requirement, and **no State is required to defray any cost by reason of it** under section 1311(d)(3)(B) of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act.\n\n**(c) SELF-INSURED PLANS INCLUDED.** \u2014 This section applies to a group health plan whether or not the plan is insured.\n\n**(d) THE SAME BARRIERS DO NOT APPLY HERE EITHER.** \u2014 Section 19-1A(h-1) and section 19-1A(a-1) apply to every plan and issuer described in subsection (a). **A plan may not deny services covered under this section on the ground that they are custodial, maintenance, long-term or non-skilled, nor apply a visit, hour or episode limit, a homebound condition, a prior-stay requirement, or an expectation of improvement.**\n\n**(e) THE THREE PLACES.** \u2014 This section is enacted as a requirement of the Public Health Service Act, of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, **so that it applies identically to insured coverage, to self-insured group health plans, and to coverage purchased through an Exchange.**\n\n**(f) FLOOR, NOT CEILING.** \u2014 Nothing in this section limits a plan or issuer from covering services at home more broadly, or a State from requiring it."},{"anchor":"sec-19-2","heading":"\u00a7 19-2. Home and community-based services become a required benefit","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE AMENDMENT.** \u2014 Section 1902(a)(10)(A) and section 1905(a) of the Social Security Act are amended so that home and community-based services are included in the medical assistance a State plan must provide to an individual who meets the criteria stated in section 19-4.\n\n**(b) THE WAIVER ARCHITECTURE IS RETIRED \u2014 BUT NOT ALL OF IT.** \u2014 The authorities under sections 1915(c), 1915(j) and 1915(k) of that Act are repealed on the date stated in the schedule.\n\n**(b-2) SECTION 1915(i) IS PRESERVED, AND DELIBERATELY.** \u2014 Section 1915(i) of that Act is not repealed. **A State may continue to furnish services under that section, and the requirements of this component are a floor beneath it, not a ceiling over it.**\n\n**(b-3) THE GENERAL RULE APPLIES HERE TOO.** \u2014 Sections 19-1A(a-1) and 19-1A(h-1) apply to a State plan under title XIX, and a State may not apply to services furnished at home any exclusion, limitation, or criterion described in those subsections.\n\n**(c) TRANSITION.** \u2014 Until the date stated in subsection (b), a State operating under a repealed authority continues to do so. **No individual receiving services on that date may experience an interruption, a reduction, or a redetermination by reason of the transition**, and services furnished under a repealed authority are deemed to satisfy this section during the transition.\n\n**(d) THE SOLE CONSEQUENCE OF A FAILURE UNDER THIS COMPONENT.** \u2014 Where a State fails to comply with a requirement of this component, the only consequence is withholding of the increased Federal medical assistance percentage provided by section 19-6. **No other payment to the State under title XIX may be withheld, reduced, deferred, or disallowed by reason of a failure under this component**, and no such failure is a ground for disapproving a State plan.\n\n**(e) CLEAR STATEMENT.** \u2014 The conditions stated in this component are conditions on the increased payment provided by section 19-6, are stated unambiguously, and apply only to conduct occurring after the date stated in the schedule. **A State accepting that payment accepts these conditions and no others.**"},{"anchor":"sec-19-3","heading":"\u00a7 19-3. Furnished within a stated time \u2014 and time is what is measured","part":null,"body":"**(a) ENTITLEMENT.** \u2014 An individual who meets the criteria stated in section 19-4 is entitled to home and community-based services, and shall receive them.\n\n**(b) THE CLOCK.** \u2014 Services shall commence within the period stated in the schedule, beginning on the date of the request. **The measure is the interval between the request and the commencement of services for each individual.**\n\n**(c) TIME, NOT A LIST.** \u2014 Compliance under this section is determined by the intervals described in subsection (b), **and not by the existence, length, composition, or maintenance of any list.** A State that keeps no list, that maintains an interest list rather than a waiting list, or that removes an individual from a list is measured identically.\n\n**(d) WHAT MAY STOP THE CLOCK, AND THESE ONLY.** \u2014 The period under subsection (b) is tolled only \u2014\n\n**(1)** for a period during which the individual is receiving inpatient care;\n\n**(2)** for a period requested by the individual; or\n\n**(3)** for the period, not exceeding the period stated in the schedule, reasonably required to complete an assessment under section 19-4 where the individual has not furnished information the individual possesses and has been asked for.\n\n**No other circumstance tolls the period. The omission of a circumstance from this subsection is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(e) THE ROUTES BY WHICH THIS WOULD OTHERWISE BE DEFEATED, CLOSED.** \u2014 A State may not, by any means, limit the number of individuals receiving services, the geographic areas in which services are available, or the groups of individuals eligible for them. This subsection applies to \u2014\n\n**(1)** a cap, quota, reservation, or allocation of slots;\n\n**(2)** a limit on expenditure per individual, or in the aggregate, operating to deny or reduce services an individual's assessment establishes as needed;\n\n**(3)** a limitation of services to particular counties, regions, or categories of disability or diagnosis;\n\n**(4)** a restriction on the number, capacity, or geographic distribution of enrolled providers; and\n\n**(5)** any other measure achieving substantially the result described in this subsection, **regardless of its form, title, or characterization**, and whether it operates through eligibility, assessment, payment, enrollment, or procurement.\n\n**The enumeration in this subsection is illustrative and not limiting.**\n\n**(f) PUBLICATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, by State and not less often than quarterly, the distribution of intervals under subsection (b), including the median and the ninetieth percentile; **the number of requests received, the number for which services commenced, the number withdrawn before services commenced, and the number pending beyond the period stated in the schedule**; and **the causes of delay, stated as the number of periods tolled under each paragraph of subsection (d), the number of assessments not completed, and the number of individuals for whom no worker or provider was available.** **A pattern of intervals exceeding the period stated in the schedule is evidence of inadequate staffing of the services required by this component, and shall be stated as such in the publication.** Publication is a ministerial duty, enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, and no discretion attaches to it.\n\n**(g) WHERE THE PERIOD IS MISSED, THE CONSEQUENCE IS INVESTMENT, NOT WITHDRAWAL.** \u2014 Where a State's intervals under subsection (b) exceed the period stated in the schedule, **the increased Federal medical assistance percentage under section 19-6 is not withheld.** The State shall instead direct an amount equal to the portion stated in the schedule into direct care worker compensation, recruitment, and retention, and shall report what it spent and on what.\n\n**(g-2) THE CORRECTOR \u2014 WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY FIX IT.** \u2014 Where the publication under subsection (f) shows a State's intervals exceeding the period stated in the schedule, the Secretary shall state in the same publication, for that State, **the number of direct care workers, assessors and case managers required to meet the period**, and shall transmit that statement to the Congress. The statement is renewed in each publication until the period is met.\n\n**(h) AND WHERE THE PERIOD IS MISSED, THE INDIVIDUAL DOES NOT KEEP WAITING.** \u2014 Where the period stated in the schedule elapses without services commencing, and the period is not tolled under subsection (d), **the individual may obtain the assessed services from any willing provider and be reimbursed at the rate applicable under section 33-5**, and the fiscal intermediary required by section 19-9(b) shall be furnished for that purpose. **No amount so paid is recoverable from the individual**, and nothing in this subsection reduces, conditions or delays any other obligation under this component. **Reimbursement is claimed and paid through the process the applicable payer already operates for a claim for a covered service** \u2014 section 19-1A(f-1)'s machinery, and no new tribunal, proceeding, or cause of action is created by this subsection. **An amount received under this subsection is a payment for medical care, is not includible in the individual's gross income, and is not counted as income or as a resource in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal or federally assisted benefit conditioned on need**."},{"anchor":"sec-19-4","heading":"\u00a7 19-4. Eligibility is locked to the institutional standard","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE LOCK.** \u2014 An individual meets the criteria of this section if the individual meets the level-of-care criteria the State applies for admission to a nursing facility or an intermediate care facility for individuals with intellectual disabilities. **The criteria are the same criteria. A State may not apply to home and community-based services any criterion, threshold, assessment instrument, or scoring method it does not apply to institutional admission.**\n\n**(a-1) AND THE LOCK IS A ONE-WAY RATCHET.** \u2014 The criteria applied under subsection (a) may not be more restrictive than those the State applied **on the date of introduction of this Act**.\n\n**(b) FINANCIAL ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 The financial eligibility rules applicable to an individual seeking home and community-based services are those applicable to an individual seeking institutional care, and no other.\n\n**(d) NO REASSESSMENT PENALTY.** \u2014 An individual receiving services may not be reassessed more often than the period stated in the schedule, and a reassessment may not reduce services except on a finding, stated in writing and furnished to the individual, that the individual's assessed need has changed.\n\n**(e) THE ASSESSOR MAY NOT BE THE PROVIDER.** \u2014 An assessment under this section may not be performed by an entity with a financial interest in the services assessed, or by an entity under common control with such an entity.\n\n**(f) ONE ASSESSMENT, HONORED BY EVERY PAYER.** \u2014 An assessment performed by any payer described in section 19-1A(b) shall be accepted by every other such payer, for the period stated in the schedule, without repetition."},{"anchor":"sec-19-5","heading":"\u00a7 19-5. The choice of setting belongs to the individual","part":null,"body":"**(a) CHOICE.** \u2014 An individual eligible for both institutional care and home and community-based services is entitled to choose between them, and to change that choice.\n\n**(b) NO PROVISION OF THIS COMPONENT REQUIRES ANY INDIVIDUAL TO LEAVE AN INSTITUTION, TO RECEIVE SERVICES AT HOME, OR TO ACCEPT A FAMILY CAREGIVER.** A State may not close, deny admission to, or reduce a payment for institutional care by reason of this component.\n\n**(c) TRANSITION SUPPORTED.** \u2014 Where an individual in an institution chooses to receive services at home, the State shall furnish transition assistance, including the costs stated in the schedule of establishing a household.\n\n**(d) THE CHOICE IS INFORMED.** \u2014 At admission to an institution, and annually thereafter, an individual shall be informed in writing of the availability of home and community-based services and of the right under subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-19-6","heading":"\u00a7 19-6. Federal payment","part":null,"body":"**(a) INCREASED PERCENTAGE.** \u2014 The Federal medical assistance percentage for home and community-based services is increased by the number of percentage points stated in the schedule.\n\n**(b) WHAT THE PAYMENT IS KEYED TO.** \u2014 The increase applies to expenditures for services actually furnished to individuals. **It is not keyed to a State's aggregate long-term care spending, to its rate of growth, or to any measure a State computes about itself.**\n\n**(c) ONE WAY.** \u2014 The increased percentage may be withheld only prospectively, only after notice identifying the requirement not met and the facts on which that rests, and only for the period during which the failure continues. **Amounts already paid may not be recovered by reason of a failure under this component.**\n\n**(d) MAINTENANCE OF EFFORT.** \u2014 A State may not reduce the scope, amount, or duration of services furnished, or the eligibility for them, below what it furnished **on the date of introduction of this Act**.\n\n**(e) NO SUPPLANTING.** \u2014 The increase may not be used to reduce State expenditure for services described in this component."},{"anchor":"sec-19-7","heading":"\u00a7 19-7. Direct care workers","part":null,"body":"**(a) COMPENSATION PASS-THROUGH.** \u2014 Of the payment a provider receives for personal care, attendant, homemaker, home health aide, and habilitation services, the percentage stated in the schedule shall be paid as compensation to direct care workers furnishing those services.\n\n**(b) THE CONSEQUENCE IS PROPORTIONAL, NOT ALL-OR-NOTHING.** \u2014 Where a provider's compensation share falls below the percentage under subsection (a), the payment to that provider is reduced in proportion to the shortfall. No payment is denied in full by reason of a shortfall under this section.\n\n**(c) COMPENSATION, CLOSED.** \u2014 Compensation means wages, salary, employer payroll taxes, and employer contributions to health, retirement, and paid leave benefits, paid with respect to a direct care worker. **It does not include training costs, supervision, travel reimbursement, administrative overhead, staffing agency margin, recruitment expense, or any payment to a person other than the direct care worker** \u2014 regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the payment.\n\n**(d) SELF-DIRECTED ARRANGEMENTS.** \u2014 Subsection (a) does not apply where the individual receiving services sets the worker's rate of pay.\n\n**(e) WAGE FLOOR.** \u2014 A direct care worker shall be paid not less than the amount stated in the schedule, which shall be adjusted annually by the same index used for the payment rates under this component. **This is a floor, and compensation is continuous above it \u2014 no step arises at the floor.**\n\n**(f) RETAINER PAYMENTS.** \u2014 A State shall pay a provider to hold a worker's assignment during a period in which the individual is hospitalized or absent, for the period stated in the schedule.\n\n**(g) TRAVEL TIME IS COMPENSATION.** \u2014 Time spent traveling between individuals is compensation for purposes of subsection (c), and shall be paid.\n\n**(h) A COMMUNITY-RECRUITED WORKFORCE PATHWAY.** \u2014 A State may meet any part of its obligations under this component by contracting with an entity serving a defined community to recruit, train, employ and supervise direct care workers **from that community**, and payment to such an entity is payment for services under this component.\n\n**(i) THIS IS A STATUTE.** \u2014 The requirements of this section apply notwithstanding any regulation, and are not affected by the amendment, rescission, or invalidation of any regulation addressing the same subject."},{"anchor":"sec-19-8","heading":"\u00a7 19-8. Family caregivers","part":null,"body":"**(a) PAYMENT PERMITTED.** \u2014 A State shall permit an individual to select a family caregiver as a paid provider of services the individual is assessed to need, and shall pay that caregiver at the rate applicable to any other provider of the same service.\n\n**(a-1) OR A STIPEND, WHICH IS SIMPLER.** \u2014 In place of payment under subsection (a), a State shall offer the individual the option of a monthly stipend paid to the family caregiver, **set by the caregiver's assessed level of care rather than by hours worked**, together with respite under subsection (d) and, where the caregiver is not otherwise covered, enrollment in health coverage.\n\n**(b) SPOUSES AND LEGALLY RESPONSIBLE RELATIVES INCLUDED.** \u2014 A State may not exclude a caregiver from payment by reason of marriage to, or legal responsibility for, the individual receiving services.\n\n**(c) NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR THE ASSESSMENT.** \u2014 The availability of a family caregiver may not reduce the services an individual is assessed to need, and may not be a ground for denying, delaying, or reducing services.\n\n**(c-1) THE CHOICE IS CONFIRMED PRIVATELY.** \u2014 At the assessment and at each reassessment, the individual's needs and the individual's choice of caregiver shall be confirmed **outside the presence of the caregiver**.\n\n**(d) RESPITE.** \u2014 An individual receiving care from an unpaid family caregiver is entitled to respite services in the amount stated in the schedule.\n\n**(e) TAX CHARACTER, STATED.** \u2014 A payment to a family caregiver under this section is a difficulty of care payment excludable from gross income under section 131 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, **whether or not the caregiver resides in the same home as the individual**, and section 131 is amended accordingly.\n\n**(f) MEANS-TESTED PROGRAM NEUTRALITY.** \u2014 A payment under this section shall not be counted as income or as a resource in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal or federally assisted benefit that is conditioned on need.\n\n**(g) CAREGIVER ASSESSMENT.** \u2014 On request, a family caregiver is entitled to an assessment of the caregiver's own needs, and to referral to services identified by it."},{"anchor":"sec-19-9","heading":"\u00a7 19-9. Self-direction","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE OPTION.** \u2014 An individual is entitled to select, train, schedule, supervise, and dismiss the workers furnishing the individual's services, and to determine how the individual's assessed budget is allocated among services.\n\n**(b) SUPPORT.** \u2014 A State shall furnish a fiscal intermediary and the counseling necessary to exercise the option, at no cost to the individual.\n\n**(c) NOT A CONDITION.** \u2014 Self-direction is an option, and no individual may be required to exercise it or disadvantaged for declining it."},{"anchor":"sec-19-10","heading":"\u00a7 19-10. Applying, and being found eligible","part":null,"body":"**(a) ONE DOOR, ANY DOOR.** \u2014 A State shall accept a request at any office, agency, or channel through which it accepts an application for medical assistance, and shall not require an individual to identify the correct program or authority.\n\n**(b) PRESUMPTIVE ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 Where an assessment is not complete within the period stated in the schedule, the individual is presumptively eligible and services shall commence, subject to reconciliation on completion of the assessment. **Services furnished during presumptive eligibility are not recoverable from the individual.**\n\n**(c) DISCHARGE FROM AN INSTITUTION.** \u2014 An individual receiving institutional care is presumptively eligible for home and community-based services without a further level-of-care determination."},{"anchor":"sec-19-11","heading":"\u00a7 19-11. Reporting","part":null,"body":"**(a) BY THE STATE.** \u2014 A State shall report the intervals required by section 19-3(b), the compensation shares required by section 19-7, and the services furnished, in the form the Secretary prescribes.\n\n**(b) THE INDIVIDUAL'S OWN RECORD.** \u2014 A State shall furnish to each individual, on request and without charge, the date of the individual's request and the date services commenced. **The individual's record is evidence of those dates in any proceeding**, and the absence of a State record of a request does not defeat a request the individual establishes.\n\n**(c) NO NEW COLLECTION.** \u2014 This section authorizes the collection of no information not already required to administer the benefit."},{"anchor":"sec-19-12","heading":"\u00a7 19-12. Rule of construction","part":null,"body":"Nothing in this component limits any right under the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990 or under section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, or affects the construction of either."},{"anchor":"sec-19-13","heading":"\u00a7 19-13. Home-based primary care","part":null,"body":"**(a) COVERED SERVICE.** \u2014 Home-based primary care furnished to an individual who meets the criteria of section 19-4 is a covered service under each payer described in section 19-1A(b).\n\n**(b) WHAT IT IS.** \u2014 Continuing primary care furnished in the individual's home by an interdisciplinary team including a physician or advanced practice clinician, a registered nurse, and a social worker, and including as the individual's needs require a pharmacist, a therapist, a dietitian, and a behavioral health clinician.\n\n**(c) WHAT IT INCLUDES.** \u2014 Comprehensive assessment, ongoing medical management, medication review, coordination with any other clinician treating the individual, and **availability of the team at all hours**.\n\n**(d) PAID FOR MANAGING A PERSON, NOT FOR VISITS.** \u2014 Payment under this section is made for managing the individual over a period, and **is not conditioned on the number of encounters, their duration, or their location within the home**.\n\n**(e) EFFECT ON THE COMPARISON UNDER SECTION 19-1A.** \u2014 Where an individual's plan of care includes services under this section, the comparison under section 19-1A(c) is made on that basis. **The cost of care at home is the cost of the plan actually proposed, including clinical management, and not the cost of attendant hours alone.**"},{"anchor":"sec-19-14","heading":"\u00a7 19-14. Safety in the home \u2014 incidents, investigation, and what is published","part":null,"body":"**(a) REPORTABLE INCIDENT.** \u2014 A death, a serious injury, an act of abuse, neglect, or exploitation, an unexplained absence, a medication error resulting in harm, or a use of restraint, occurring with respect to an individual receiving services under this component.\n\n**(b) DUTY TO REPORT.** \u2014 A provider furnishing services under this component shall report a reportable incident to the State within the period stated in the schedule. **A failure to report is subject to section 19-7's payment consequence on the same terms.**\n\n**(c) THE INDIVIDUAL MAY REPORT DIRECTLY.** \u2014 An individual, a family member, or any other person may report a reportable incident directly to the State. **No provider may retaliate against any person for making a report, and a term of any agreement purporting to restrict reporting is void.**\n\n**(d) DUTY TO INVESTIGATE.** \u2014 The State shall investigate each reportable incident within the period stated in the schedule, and shall record the finding.\n\n**(e) PUBLICATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, by State and by provider type, the number of reportable incidents, the number investigated, and the time to investigation. **Publication is a ministerial duty**, and no individual may be identified.\n\n**(f) AN INCIDENT IS NOT A REASON TO INSTITUTIONALIZE THE PERSON.** \u2014 A reportable incident, and any finding on it, **may not be a ground for terminating or reducing an individual's services, for denying a request to receive services at home, or for requiring a change of setting.** **The response runs against the provider. It does not run against the individual.**\n\n**(g) NO SECOND APPARATUS.** \u2014 Where a State already operates an adult protective services system, a licensing system, or an incident management system reaching the conduct described in subsection (a), this section is satisfied by that system, **and this section creates no new inspectorate**."},{"anchor":"sec-19-15","heading":"\u00a7 19-15. The home safety and function intervention","part":null,"body":"**(a) COVERED SERVICE.** \u2014 On referral, a course of services consisting of assessment and training by a registered nurse and an occupational therapist, together with modification of the dwelling and provision of assistive devices, furnished over the period stated in the schedule.\n\n**(b) THE MODIFICATION BUDGET.** \u2014 Up to the amount stated in the schedule per individual for each course under subsection (a), for changes to the dwelling and devices identified by the assessment as necessary to the individual's function or safety.\n\n**(c) NOT COUNTED AGAINST ANYTHING ELSE.** \u2014 Services under this section are not counted against any limit, budget, or assessed amount applicable to other services under this component.\n\n**(d) NOT CONDITIONED ON INSTITUTIONAL ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 This section applies to an individual with difficulty performing activities of daily living, **whether or not the individual meets the criteria of section 19-4.**"},{"anchor":"sec-19-16","heading":"\u00a7 19-16. Administration \u2014 what pays for the people who deliver this","part":null,"body":"**(a) WHAT THIS SECTION REACHES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.** \u2014 **The services themselves are paid for where they always were**: medical assistance under title XIX, at the percentage section 19-6 sets, and after the transition date under section 33-1, by the program. This section reaches **the administration** \u2014 the assessing, the case management, the fiscal intermediary, and the Secretary's own publication duties \u2014 **which no provision of this Act had funded, and which is the work that converts an entitlement into a service that arrives.**\n\n**(b) PERMANENT APPROPRIATION.** \u2014 There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for the Secretary to perform the publication required by sections 19-3(f), 19-3(g-2) and 19-14(e), to designate and maintain the assessment instrument under section 33-4, and to administer this component. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated **permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.**\n\n**(c) AND IT COVERS PEOPLE, NAMED.** \u2014 The appropriation made by subsection (b) **covers personnel** \u2014 the staff who compile and publish the intervals and the causes, and the staff who maintain the instrument \u2014 and is not confined to systems, contracts or overhead.\n\n**(d) THE DELIVERING STAFF ARE FUNDED AT THE RATE THE SERVICE IS.** \u2014 Expenditures by a State for **assessment under section 19-4 and for the fiscal intermediary and counseling required by section 19-9(b)** are expenditures for home and community-based services for purposes of section 19-6(a), and the increased percentage applies to them.\n\n**(e) EXCEPTED PERSONNEL, AND PAYMENT THROUGH A LAPSE.** \u2014 The **officers and employees of the United States** necessary to perform the activities in subsection (b) shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the shutdown plan of the department concerned, and that identification published. **State personnel are not subject to a federal furlough and this subsection does not purport to reach them** \u2014 what reaches them is the money: **payment to a State under section 19-6, including for the expenditures described in subsection (d), continues during a lapse in appropriations without interruption**.\n\n**(f) NO DISPLACEMENT.** \u2014 Amounts appropriated under this section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the Secretary or to any State for any other purpose. **No function performed before enactment shall be reduced, delayed, or discontinued in order to carry out this Act.**\n\n**(g) NO OFF SWITCH.** \u2014 No duty under this component is conditioned on an appropriation, and the absence or insufficiency of any other appropriation does not suspend, toll, or diminish any duty under it. **A defense that a duty was not effective, or was excused, for want of funds is not available in any action under this component.** **This subsection addresses appropriations and nothing else**; the obligations of a State remain conditions on its acceptance of the increased payment under section 19-2(e), and nothing in this section requires a State to accept that payment or to administer this component."},{"anchor":"sec-26-1","heading":"\u00a7 26-1. Incontinence supplies","part":null,"body":"Incontinence supplies are covered for an individual with a medical condition or functional limitation requiring them, on prescription or plan-of-care listing, per the schedule."},{"anchor":"sec-26-2","heading":"\u00a7 26-2. Respite, and the wraparound tier","part":null,"body":"**(a) RESPITE.** \u2014 Respite care \u2014 substitute care relieving a primary caregiver \u2014 is covered for an individual with a qualifying need under the National Needs Assessment (\u00a7 33-4), for not less than **sixteen hours per month**, in-home or facility-based at the family's choice.\n\n**(b) THE WRAPAROUND TIER.** \u2014 For an individual whose assessed needs meet the tier standard in the schedule, the program covers the ECHO class of supports: assistive technology and devices, medical supplies, training of family members in the individual's care, and expanded in-home services."},{"anchor":"sec-26-3","heading":"\u00a7 26-3. Family caregivers may be paid, and the home may be a foster home","part":null,"body":"**(a)** \u00a7 19-9 (self-direction) is amended to state expressly: **a self-directed budget may employ any individual of the participant's choosing, including a family member, a spouse, or a parent**, at rates within the budget, **with electronic visit verification applying as the integrity instrument \u2014 the requirement of section 1903(l) of the Social Security Act as in effect on the date of introduction, re-enacted as a rule of this section, so that the repeal of title XIX does not carry the integrity machinery away with the program it policed.**\n\n**(b)** For the highest-need tier, the schedule provides a **caregiver stipend** on the PCAFC model \u2014 a fixed monthly amount in lieu of hourly employment, at the household's election.\n\n**(c) MEDICAL FOSTER HOME.** \u2014 Care in a qualified medical foster home \u2014 a caregiver's private residence providing 24-hour care to no more than the number of residents set in the schedule \u2014 is a covered institutional alternative at the individual's election, on the VA program's model, at a rate not exceeding the facility rate it displaces."},{"anchor":"sec-26-4","heading":"\u00a7 26-4. Travel \u2014 the two triggers, one title","part":null,"body":"**(a) REFERRED-CARE TRAVEL (DISTANCE-GATED).** \u2014 Where a covered individual is referred for care unavailable within the access interval of section 6-1(c) of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act or beyond the mileage threshold set in the schedule, reasonable travel, lodging, and meal costs for the individual and one attendant are covered, per the schedule.\n\n**(b) TRANSFER TRAVEL HOME \u2014 UNIVERSAL.** \u2014 Where an individual is transferred between hospitals, the individual's insurer \u2014 **this program, a plan under the Public Option Act, or a group health plan alike** **enacted for plans through the single rulebook** \u2014 shall pay the cost of the individual's return home, **with no cost sharing and without regard to income**:\n\n**(1) THE AMOUNT.** \u2014 For private-vehicle travel, mileage at the standard business rate most recently published by the Internal Revenue Service, for the reimbursable distance: **the lesser of (A) the distance from the transferring hospital to the receiving hospital, and (B) the distance from the receiving hospital to the individual's home** \u2014 and where a person drives to retrieve the individual, **twice that distance**, both legs of the retrieval. For commercial or arranged transport \u2014 however arranged \u2014 the actual cost; for medical transport where indicated, the covered amount.\n\n**(2) PROVISION OR REIMBURSEMENT.** \u2014 The insurer discharges the duty by reimbursing, or by furnishing the transport directly, including under standing contracts; **an arranged option satisfies the duty only if furnished timely, and an individual whose arranged transport fails or is materially delayed may self-arrange and be reimbursed.**\n\n**(3) NO INCOME TEST, STATED AS THE EXCEPTION IT IS.** \u2014 This subsection is a deliberate exception to income scaling: **the burden is transfer-imposed, and no one pays more because the system moved them.**\n\n**(4)** Where discharge is to a place other than home, that place substitutes; successive transfers apply paragraph (1) per leg, with the home cap at final discharge."},{"anchor":"sec-26-5","heading":"\u00a7 26-5. Supported employment","part":null,"body":"Individual placement and support \u2014 evidence-based supported employment for individuals with serious mental illness or a qualifying disability \u2014 is covered for individuals meeting the need standard in the schedule."},{"anchor":"sec-26-6","heading":"\u00a7 26-6. Food as treatment, and the home as a treatment site","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Medically tailored meals are covered, **as treatment**: for an individual with a qualifying condition listed in the schedule, on clinician referral, for the period the referral states within the schedule maximum, renewable on re-referral.\n\n**(b)** \u00a7 19-15 (the home safety and function intervention) is amended to name **asthma-trigger remediation** \u2014 air filtration, ventilation, mold and pest removal \u2014 among its covered interventions."},{"anchor":"sec-33-1","heading":"\u00a7 33-1. The benefit, and the re-key of \u00a7 19","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE BENEFIT.** \u2014 Long-term services and supports \u2014 the services of \u00a7 19-1(a), institutional care under \u00a7 33-6(c), and the \u00a7 26 additions \u2014 are benefits of the program, furnished according to need assessed under \u00a7 33-4, with the individual's subsidy determined under \u00a7 33-2.\n\n**(b) THE RE-KEY, STATED AS RULES.** \u2014 On the transition date, within component 19 \u2014\n\n**(1)** a duty of \"the State\" is a duty of the Center; a State-plan requirement is a program rule; the \u00a7 19-3 clock runs against the Center, **with its publication and causes of delay (\u00a7 19-3(f)), its tolling list, its investment-not-withdrawal consequence, its corrector (\u00a7 19-3(g-2)) and the individual's self-arrangement right (\u00a7 19-3(h)) intact** (the workforce direction of \u00a7 19-3(g) now funding \u00a7 19-7's provisions directly), and \u00a7 19-16's administration appropriation and excepted-personnel designation apply to the Center;\n\n**(2)** \u00a7\u00a7 19-2 and 19-6 \u2014 the State-plan amendment and FMAP machinery \u2014 **are superseded by this Title and expire with title XIX** (their protective content survives: \u00a7 19-6(d)'s maintenance rule becomes \u00a7 33-6(d)'s floor; \u00a7 19-2(b-2)'s below-institutional population is served by construction, because \u00a7 33-4's continuous score has no institutional threshold to fall below);\n\n**(3)** \u00a7 19-4's institutional-standard lock is **succeeded by \u00a7 33-4, with its floor preserved**: any individual who would have met any State's institutional level-of-care criteria as of the date of introduction is within the benefit \u2014 the assessment may reach further; it may never reach less (the one-way ratchet of \u00a7 19-4(a-1), nationalized); and\n\n**(4)** \u00a7 19-1A, \u00a7 19-1C, and every payer-neutral rule persist unchanged \u2014 they bind group plans and issuers through the single rulebook, and this Title adds the program's own benefit above that floor."},{"anchor":"sec-33-2","heading":"\u00a7 33-2. The gate \u2014 the median worker, fully covered","part":null,"body":"**(a)** The LTSS subsidy is **full** \u2014 no premium and no cost sharing for assessed services \u2014 for an individual whose household income does not exceed the median-wage level: **the 50th-percentile hourly wage published under section 1-4(b)(2) of the People's Prosperity Act, annualized and converted to a household measure as the schedule provides, at the vintage most recently published as of the determination** \u2014 a later revision of the series does not reopen a determination (\u00a7 17-3's one-way rule) \u2014 and **phases continuously** above it under the \u00a7 17 schedule's rules, including \u00a7 17-5(b)'s no-cliffs rule of law.\n\n**(b)** The spending-deepener of \u00a7 17-4 applies: assessed-service costs incurred above the schedule share of income move the individual down the schedule for the year.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this section conditions the *benefit* \u2014 assessment and services follow need at every income; income moves only the price (\u00a7 20-2's principle, applied to long-term care)."},{"anchor":"sec-33-3","heading":"\u00a7 33-3. The asset test \u2014 only here, and gamable by no one","part":null,"body":"**(a)** For the LTSS subsidy only \u2014 **the ordinary schedule runs income-only** \u2014 countable resources above the schedule threshold **phase the subsidy continuously** \u2014 no eligibility cliff, no spend-down ritual: each dollar of countable resources above the threshold adjusts the subsidy by the schedule's continuous factor.\n\n**(b) LOOK-THROUGH, THE CONTROL-IN-FACT FORM.** \u2014 Countable resources include assets held through, or transferred for less than value to, a trust, entity, or arrangement the individual or spouse funded or controls in fact \u2014 **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the arrangement** the schedule states the lookback and the exemptions (the home to the schedule value, retirement flows as income not resource, and a Prosperity Account under the People's Prosperity Act to the amount disregarded by section 3-9(b-2) of that Act \u2014 a balance above that amount is countable under this section and phases the subsidy continuously, and an account is in no case reached by a lookback, a transfer penalty, or a recovery).\n\n**(c)** Determination rides \u00a7 17-3's machinery \u2014 attestation co-equal, one-way within period, audit not prior-approval \u2014 **and estate recovery does not exist**."},{"anchor":"sec-33-4","heading":"\u00a7 33-4. The National Needs Assessment","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE INSTRUMENT.** \u2014 Need is measured by the National Needs Assessment: a validated instrument of the interRAI class designated by publication, **whose statutory rules are these** \u2014\n\n**(1) THE DOMAINS**: physical function; cognition; behavior and psychiatric need; medical complexity; sensory and communication; and the supports required for community living \u2014 enumerated here, weighted so that cognitive and behavioral need count fully, and **narrowable by no agency** (no rulemaking authority exists over this subsection);\n\n**(2) PERSON-INTRINSIC**: assessed need is measured **without regard to care any family member or other person furnishes or might furnish**;\n\n**(3) CONTINUOUS**: the output is a continuous score mapping to a continuously scaled benefit and self-directed budget \u2014 **no grade, level, or band may cause an increment of measured need to produce a benefit step larger than itself**;\n\n**(4) COMMUNITY IS THE MEASURE**: the instrument assesses supports needed to live in the community; **no score, output, or determination routes any individual toward an institution** (\u00a7 19-5's choice architecture governs settings).\n\n**(b) CONFLICT-FREE, BY STATUTE.** \u2014 \u00a7 19-4(e)'s assessor-independence rule, elevated: the assessor may not be, be employed by, or be compensated by a provider of services assessed, and inter-rater reliability is published.\n\n**(c) THE BENEFIT NEVER WAITS.** \u2014 \u00a7 19-10(b)'s presumptive rule, generalized: a clinician's attestation of evident need commences a provisional benefit immediately, trued one-way (nothing recovered from the individual); the schedule's deemed classes skip to provisional; **no backlog, system, or vacancy delays care** (\u00a7 24's architecture, applied here).\n\n**(d) REVISION IS ONE-WAY AT THE PERSON LEVEL.** \u2014 A revised or re-designated instrument never reduces an individual's existing benefit absent a \u00a7 19-4(d)-form written finding of changed circumstances.\n\n**(e) PUBLISHED TO RECONSTRUCTION.** \u2014 The instrument, scoring, revision criteria, and jurisdiction-level distributions are published (\u00a7 16-4 form) \u2014 **a public instrument, never a black box.**\n\n**(f) ONE ASSESSMENT, EVERY PAYER.** \u2014 \u00a7 19-4(f), carried forward program-wide."},{"anchor":"sec-33-5","heading":"\u00a7 33-5. Rates","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Services under this Title are paid on **national schedules, geographically adjusted by the single geographic wage adjustment set in the schedule**: a single unified prospective payment schedule for post-acute stays, with classes and rates set in the schedule; national fee schedules for HCBS service classes; and the \u00a7 19-7 worker provisions (pass-through, wage floor, travel time, retainers) applying to every payment.\n\n**(b) THE ONE-WAY TRANSITION.** \u2014 Where the national schedule exceeds a legacy rate, the national schedule applies immediately; where it is lower, the legacy rate is **frozen in nominal terms until the national schedule reaches it**."},{"anchor":"sec-33-6","heading":"\u00a7 33-6. Structure rules","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE BENEFIT RAMPS; PEOPLE ARE NEVER SEQUENCED.** \u2014 Home and community services are payable from this Title's first effective date; institutional-benefit integration and the full subsidy schedule complete by the scheduled steps **the German home-first stagger \u2014 a ramp of the benefit, never a queue of people; every eligible person is in from day one at the then-current benefit level**.\n\n**(b) NO ACTUARIAL GATE, NO OPT-OUT WINDOW.** \u2014 No certification, solvency finding, or actuarial determination conditions this Title's operation (reports are monitors; \u00a7 30-5-form correction belongs to Congress); and **no private-coverage opt-out from its financing exists**.\n\n**(c) INSTITUTIONAL CARE.** \u2014 Covered per \u00a7 19-5's choice rules: the funding default is community, the person's choice governs, and \u00a7 19-1A(i)'s one-way rules protect the institutional choice from the comparison.\n\n**(d) THE FLOOR.** \u2014 No individual receiving services on the transition date experiences reduction, interruption, or reassessment by reason of this Title (\u00a7 19-2(c)'s form; \u00a7 21-1(c) holds the interim)."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 19-1A(g) band by which the cost of care at home may exceed the cost of the institutional services | 100 percent of the cost of the institutional services | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 19-2(b) date on which sections 1915(c), 1915(j) and 1915(k) are repealed | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 19-2(e) date after which the conditions of the component apply | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 19-3(b) period within which services commence after the request | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 19-3(d)(3) maximum tolling for an assessment awaiting information the individual possesses | 10 days in total for each request, counted from the date the State first asked for the information | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 19-3(g) portion directed into direct care worker compensation, recruitment and retention | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 19-4(d) minimum interval between reassessments | 12 months, except on the individual's request; a request made on a form furnished as a condition of receiving services is not a request | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 19-4(f) period for which an assessment is honored by every payer | 12 months from the date of the assessment | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 19-5(c) costs of establishing a household | Rent and utility deposits, first month's rent and utilities, bedding, basic kitchen supplies, and other necessities linked to an assessed need for the individual to move from the institution to the home | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 19-6(a) increase in the Federal medical assistance percentage | [___ percentage points] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 19-7(a) share of payment paid as direct care worker compensation | 80 percent of the payment less the costs of training required by Federal or State law, travel, and personal protective equipment for direct care workers | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 19-7(e) direct care worker wage floor | [$___ per hour] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 19-7(f) retainer period | 30 consecutive days for each hospitalization or absence | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 19-8(d) respite | Not less than 30 days in each year, each of 24 hours, or 720 hours in each year at the family's choice, including in-home care and 24-hour care, and more where the assessment establishes the need; the 16 hours per month of \u00a7 26-2(a) is not reduced | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 19-10(b) period for completing the assessment before presumptive eligibility | 30 days from the date of the request | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 19-14(b) period for a provider's report of a reportable incident | 2 hours after any worker of the provider furnishing services to the individual learns of it where the incident involves abuse or serious bodily injury; otherwise 24 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 19-14(d) period for the State's investigation and finding | First contact with the individual within 24 hours where the individual is at immediate risk and within 7 calendar days otherwise; the finding within 5 working days after the incident is reported, or within 5 working days after the State receives a record it does not control | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 19-15(a) period of the home safety and function intervention | 5 months from the first visit | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 19-15(b) modification budget per individual for each course | $20,000 for the calendar year of enactment; for each later calendar year, $20,000 multiplied by the product, over each later year through that year, of one plus that year's increase, the result rounded to the nearest $100. A year's increase is the Middle-Class Growth Rate defined in \u00a7 1-1 of the People's Prosperity Act, as most recently certified under \u00a7 1-3(a) of that Act before the year begins, or, where no value has been so certified within the 15 months before the year begins, the percentage change in the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics from the month 12 months before the most recent month for which it is published before the year begins to that most recent month, using each month's value as first published; an increase less than zero counts as zero. The amount for a course is the amount for the calendar year in which the course begins. The amount changes by operation of this entry, without action by any person, and the Secretary shall publish it before each year begins | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 26-1 quantity of incontinence supplies | The quantity the prescription or plan-of-care listing states; no other quantity or frequency limit applies | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 26-2(b) wraparound tier standard | An individual who meets the criteria of section 19-4, or who has a condition described in 32 CFR 199.5(b)(2), with the definitions of 32 CFR 199.2, as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 26-3(b) highest-need tier for the caregiver stipend | An individual who needs personal care each time the individual completes three or more activities of daily living and is fully dependent on a caregiver to complete them, or who needs supervision, protection or instruction on a continuous basis, each as scored by the instrument under section 33-4 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 26-3(b) caregiver stipend amount | Each month, one-twelfth of the annual rate of basic pay for grade GS-4, step 1, of the General Schedule, including locality pay for the area in which the individual resides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-24 | \u00a7 26-3(c) medical foster home resident cap | 3 residents receiving care | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-25 | \u00a7 26-4(a) referred-care mileage threshold | 75 miles from the location at which the referring clinician furnishes services to the individual, or, for a referral by telehealth, from the individual's location | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-26 | \u00a7 26-4(a) travel, lodging and meal amounts | Lodging and meals at the per diem rates established under 5 U.S.C. 5702(a)(1)(A) for the place of care; travel at the cost of commercial transport or at the mileage rate of section 26-4(b)(1), at the individual's election | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-27 | \u00a7 26-5 supported-employment need standard | An individual within section 26-5 who states a goal of competitive integrated employment, as defined in 29 U.S.C. 705(5); the individual is presumed able to benefit, and no other criterion applies | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-28 | \u00a7 26-6(a) qualifying conditions for medically tailored meals | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-29 | \u00a7 26-6(a) maximum period of a referral | 6 months, renewable for further periods of 6 months on re-referral | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-30 | \u00a7 33-2(a) conversion of the hourly wage to a household measure | The hourly wage multiplied by 2,087, and that product multiplied by the ratio of the poverty line under 42 U.S.C. 9902(2) for a household of the individual's size to the poverty line for a household of one | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-31 | \u00a7 33-3(a) asset threshold | The amount that 42 U.S.C. 1396r-5(f)(2)(A)(ii)(II) and (g), as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act, produce for the year ($162,660 for 2026) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-32 | \u00a7 33-3(a) continuous factor | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-33 | \u00a7 33-3(b) lookback | 36 months before the date of the determination | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-34 | \u00a7 33-3(b) home exemption | Equity in the home up to $1,130,000 for 2026, increased each year by the percentage increase in the consumer price index for all urban consumers, rounded to the nearest $1,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-35 | \u00a7 33-4(c) deemed classes | (a) An individual receiving institutional care; (b) an individual receiving home and community-based services under title XIX on the transition date; (c) an individual with an impairment in a category of 20 CFR 416.934 as in effect on the date of introduction of this Act; (d) an eligible veteran whose family caregiver receives a stipend under 38 U.S.C. 1720G(a)(3)(A)(ii)(V) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-36 | \u00a7 33-5(a) single geographic wage adjustment | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-37 | \u00a7 33-5(a) post-acute prospective payment classes and rates | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-38 | \u00a7 33-5(a) HCBS fee-schedule service classes and rates | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-39 | \u00a7 33-6(a) ramp steps | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. References to sections of related Acts","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCOPE.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to a section not contained in this Act is a reference to that section of the One Medicare Act, the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act, the Public Option Act, the Medical Care PAID Act, the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, and the People's Prosperity Act, as the context requires.\n\n**(b) IF THE RELATED ACT IS NOT IN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a section referred to under subsection (a) is not in effect, the reference applies as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c) IF THE SECTION HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a provision of this Act conditions a consequence on a report, program, payment, or determination established by a section not contained in this Act, and that section has never taken effect, **the condition is inapplicable and the remainder of the provision operates according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) THE PROGRAM.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to \"the program\" is a reference to coverage and benefits under the One Medicare Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or the application of any provision to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance are not affected.\n\n**(b) NOT ONE INTEGRATED SCHEME.** \u2014 The provisions of this Act are separate and independent. **A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme, and shall not infer from the invalidity of one provision that the Congress would not have enacted the remainder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-8","heading":"\u00a7 0-8. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a) IN GENERAL.** \u2014 Except as otherwise provided, this Act takes effect on the first day of the first plan year beginning after the date that is one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) SCHEDULE VALUES.** \u2014 Where a provision refers to an amount, period, threshold, or rate set in the schedule, that value is the value stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act."}],"asOf":"2026-11-10"}}}};
